### ===========================================================================
### security_tests.http - attack requests for Ward 42 Fix-It (Question D2)
###
### Needs the REST Client extension in VS Code (by Huachao Mao).
### Start the app first with:  flask run
###
### HOW TO USE
###   1. Click "Send Request" above request 1. In the answer, find the line
###      <input type="hidden" name="csrf_token" value="...">
###      and copy the long value.
###   2. Paste it after @csrf = on the line below (replace PASTE_TOKEN_HERE).
###      REST Client keeps the session cookie automatically, so the token
###      keeps working for the next requests (it lasts about one hour).
###   3. Click "Send Request" above each attack and take a screenshot of the
###      response. Each one should be REJECTED, as written in its comment.
### ===========================================================================

@host = http://127.0.0.1:5000
@csrf = PASTE_TOKEN_HERE


### 1. SETUP - get the report form, a session cookie and a CSRF token
GET {{host}}/report


### 2. SQL INJECTION in the cell number field
# Attack: the cell number is changed to  0821234567' OR '1'='1
# Expected: 400 BAD REQUEST. The server checks the cell number against
# ^0\d{9}$, so anything that is not 10 digits is refused. Even if it got
# through, the database layer uses parameterised queries (the ORM).
POST {{host}}/report
Content-Type: multipart/form-data; boundary=W42Boundary

--W42Boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="csrf_token"

{{csrf}}
--W42Boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="category"

Pothole
--W42Boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="street"

12 Tsamaya Road
--W42Boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="description"

Large pothole near the school gate
--W42Boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="reporter_name"

Test Resident
--W42Boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="reporter_cell"

0821234567' OR '1'='1
--W42Boundary--


### 3. SQL INJECTION in the status lookup address
# Attack: the reference is changed to  W42-0001' OR '1'='1
# Expected: 400 BAD REQUEST with "That reference does not look right".
# A reference must match ^W42-\d{4,}$ and the lookup uses an ORM filter.
GET {{host}}/status?reference=W42-0001'%20OR%20'1'='1


### 4. CROSS-SITE SCRIPTING (XSS) in the category field
# Attack: the category is changed to a <script> tag
# Expected: 400 BAD REQUEST. The category must be one of the five allowed
# values, so the script is refused and never stored.
POST {{host}}/report
Content-Type: multipart/form-data; boundary=W42Boundary

--W42Boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="csrf_token"

{{csrf}}
--W42Boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="category"

<script>alert('XSS')</script>
--W42Boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="street"

12 Tsamaya Road
--W42Boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="description"

Large pothole near the school gate
--W42Boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="reporter_name"

Test Resident
--W42Boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="reporter_cell"

0821234567
--W42Boundary--


### 5. XSS reflected back from the status lookup
# Attack: a <script> tag is sent as the reference
# Expected: 400 BAD REQUEST. Look in the answer (in the Reference number
# box): the text is shown as &lt;SCRIPT&gt;ALERT(1)... (harmless text, in
# capitals because the lookup capitalises what you type), never as a real
# <script> tag, because Jinja escapes everything it prints. The
# Content-Security-Policy header in the response also blocks inline scripts.
GET {{host}}/status?reference=%3Cscript%3Ealert(1)%3C/script%3E


### 6. MISSING CSRF TOKEN
# Attack: a form is sent with NO csrf_token, like a forged form on another website
# Expected: 400 BAD REQUEST. "Your form could not be checked, so it was not sent."
POST {{host}}/report
Content-Type: multipart/form-data; boundary=W42Boundary

--W42Boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="category"

Pothole
--W42Boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="street"

12 Tsamaya Road
--W42Boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="description"

Large pothole near the school gate
--W42Boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="reporter_name"

Test Resident
--W42Boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="reporter_cell"

0821234567
--W42Boundary--


### 7. INVALID UPLOAD - a script disguised as a picture
# Attack: a PHP script is uploaded with the name shell.png and an image content type
# Expected: 400 BAD REQUEST. The name and content type look fine, but the
# server reads the first bytes of the file and sees it is not a real
# JPEG, PNG or WebP: "That file is not a real JPEG, PNG or WebP image."
POST {{host}}/report
Content-Type: multipart/form-data; boundary=W42Boundary

--W42Boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="csrf_token"

{{csrf}}
--W42Boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="category"

Pothole
--W42Boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="street"

12 Tsamaya Road
--W42Boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="description"

Large pothole near the school gate
--W42Boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="reporter_name"

Test Resident
--W42Boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="reporter_cell"

0821234567
--W42Boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="photo"; filename="shell.png"
Content-Type: image/png

<?php echo shell_exec($_GET['cmd']); ?>
--W42Boundary--


### 8. INVALID UPLOAD - a forbidden file extension
# Attack: a file called shell.php is uploaded
# Expected: 400 BAD REQUEST. "Photos must be JPEG, PNG or WebP files."
POST {{host}}/report
Content-Type: multipart/form-data; boundary=W42Boundary

--W42Boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="csrf_token"

{{csrf}}
--W42Boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="category"

Pothole
--W42Boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="street"

12 Tsamaya Road
--W42Boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="description"

Large pothole near the school gate
--W42Boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="reporter_name"

Test Resident
--W42Boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="reporter_cell"

0821234567
--W42Boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="photo"; filename="shell.php"
Content-Type: image/png

<?php echo 'hacked'; ?>
--W42Boundary--


### 9. BROKEN ACCESS CONTROL - coordinator page without logging in
# Attack: someone who is not logged in asks for the list of all reports
# Expected: 302 FOUND, redirecting to /login. No personal data is shown.
# (Make sure you are logged out, or use a fresh VS Code window.)
GET {{host}}/reports


### 10. BROKEN ACCESS CONTROL - the JSON API without logging in
# Expected: 401 UNAUTHORIZED with {"error": "Login required"}
GET {{host}}/api/reports/summary


### 11. PATH TRAVERSAL on the photo address
# Attack: try to read a file outside the uploads folder (here, app.py)
# Expected: 404 NOT FOUND. The photo address only accepts a single file name
# (no slashes), so this request never reaches the code that reads files.
# Even if it did, send_from_directory refuses paths outside the uploads
# folder, and photos need a coordinator login anyway.
GET {{host}}/photos/..%2Fapp.py
